 "voice": [
  {
   "id": "vo01",
   "t": 0.1,
   "dur": 2.307,
   "file": "vo/01.wav",
   "text": "A bullet, through an apple, slowly."
  },
  {
   "id": "vo02",
   "t": 3.066666666666667,
   "dur": 56.963,
   "file": "vo/02.wav",
   "text": "A famous old photograph by Harold Edgerton shows a bullet going through an apple. That bullet flew at about eight hundred fifty meters a second. The apple is about eight centimeters across. So the bullet crosses it in less than a ten-thousandth of a second. Here, that takes about one second. The bullet is a small, dark shape. At this speed, you can watch it drift into the apple like a slow fish. The skin dimples. The apple has no time to react. Then the back of the apple blows outward, in a spray. Pieces of the apple trail behind it, one at a time. And the bullet is already past. In real time, that was far too quick to blink. It's moving more than twice as fast as sound, so it drags a narrow cone of air behind it. Even now, a table a meter wide would take it about twelve seconds to cross. A bullet is fast. But out the window, something is about to be much faster."
  },
  {
   "id": "vo03",
   "t": 60.833333333333336,
